<li data-start=3D"222" data-end=3D"251">
<p data-start=3D"224" data-end=3D"251"><strong data-start=3D"224" data-end=
=3D"251">Pourquoi utiliser UML ?</strong></p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start=3D"253" data-end=3D"256">
<h1 data-start=3D"258" data-end=3D"281">=F0=9F=A7=AD Introduction =C3=A0 UM=
L</h1>
<h2 data-start=3D"283" data-end=3D"311">=F0=9F=93=8C 1. Qu=E2=80=99est-ce q=
ue UML ?</h2>
<h3 data-start=3D"313" data-end=3D"334">D=C3=A9finition simple</h3>
<p data-start=3D"335" data-end=3D"596"><strong data-start=3D"335" data-end=
=3D"342">UML</strong> signifie <strong data-start=3D"352" data-end=3D"381">=
Unified Modeling Language</strong> (<em data-start=3D"383" data-end=3D"415"=
>Langage de mod=C3=A9lisation unifi=C3=A9</em>). C=E2=80=99est un <strong d=
ata-start=3D"427" data-end=3D"461">langage visuel de mod=C3=A9lisation</str=
ong> utilis=C3=A9 pour <strong data-start=3D"475" data-end=3D"550">repr=C3=
=A9senter, concevoir, documenter et comprendre des syst=C3=A8mes logiciels<=
/strong> =E2=80=94 en particulier des syst=C3=A8mes orient=C3=A9s objet.</p=
>
<p data-start=3D"598" data-end=3D"871">Ce n=E2=80=99est <strong data-start=
=3D"607" data-end=3D"641">ni un langage de programmation</strong>, ni un pr=
ocessus de d=C3=A9veloppement. UML ne permet pas d'ex=C3=A9cuter du code, m=
ais de <strong data-start=3D"723" data-end=3D"756">cr=C3=A9er des mod=C3=A8=
les conceptuels</strong>, logiques, ou physiques, qui servent de <strong da=
ta-start=3D"797" data-end=3D"846">support =C3=A0 la conception et =C3=A0 la=
 communication</strong> dans un projet logiciel.</p>
<h3 data-start=3D"873" data-end=3D"896">Ce que permet UML :</h3>
<ul data-start=3D"897" data-end=3D"1226">
<li data-start=3D"897" data-end=3D"985">
<p data-start=3D"899" data-end=3D"985">Repr=C3=A9senter la <strong data-sta=
rt=3D"914" data-end=3D"936">structure statique</strong> d=E2=80=99un syst=
=C3=A8me (classes, objets, composants, etc.)</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"986" data-end=3D"1082">
<p data-start=3D"988" data-end=3D"1082">D=C3=A9crire les <strong data-start=
=3D"1000" data-end=3D"1028">comportements dynamiques</strong> (flux d=E2=80=
=99activit=C3=A9s, transitions d=E2=80=99=C3=A9tats, interactions)</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1083" data-end=3D"1151">
<p data-start=3D"1085" data-end=3D"1151">Mod=C3=A9liser les <strong data-st=
art=3D"1099" data-end=3D"1151">interactions entre les composants ou les act=
eurs</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1152" data-end=3D"1226">
<p data-start=3D"1154" data-end=3D"1226">Fournir un <strong data-start=3D"1=
165" data-end=3D"1184">cadre normalis=C3=A9</strong> pour la conception de =
logiciels complexes</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start=3D"1228" data-end=3D"1231">
<h3 data-start=3D"1233" data-end=3D"1281">UML est un <strong data-start=3D"=
1248" data-end=3D"1279">langage graphique normalis=C3=A9</strong> :</h3>
<p data-start=3D"1282" data-end=3D"1510">Il propose une <strong data-start=
=3D"1297" data-end=3D"1322">notation standardis=C3=A9e</strong> (ic=C3=B4ne=
s, symboles, fl=C3=A8ches, blocs) qui permet =C3=A0 tous les membres d=E2=
=80=99une =C3=A9quipe technique (d=C3=A9veloppeurs, architectes, analystes =
m=C3=A9tier, testeurs, etc.) de <strong data-start=3D"1476" data-end=3D"150=
9">parler un m=C3=AAme langage visuel</strong>.</p>
<blockquote data-start=3D"1512" data-end=3D"1657">
<p data-start=3D"1514" data-end=3D"1657">=F0=9F=92=A1 Exemple : un rectangl=
e avec trois compartiments repr=C3=A9sente une classe, une fl=C3=A8che avec=
 un losange repr=C3=A9sente une relation d=E2=80=99agr=C3=A9gation, etc.</p=
>
</blockquote>
<hr data-start=3D"1659" data-end=3D"1662">
<h3 data-start=3D"1664" data-end=3D"1705">UML s=E2=80=99applique =C3=A0 dif=
f=C3=A9rents niveaux :</h3>
<ul data-start=3D"1706" data-end=3D"1910">
<li data-start=3D"1706" data-end=3D"1731">
<p data-start=3D"1708" data-end=3D"1731"><strong data-start=3D"1708" data-e=
nd=3D"1731">Analyse des besoins</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1732" data-end=3D"1757">
<p data-start=3D"1734" data-end=3D"1757"><strong data-start=3D"1734" data-e=
nd=3D"1757">Mod=C3=A9lisation m=C3=A9tier</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1758" data-end=3D"1787">
<p data-start=3D"1760" data-end=3D"1787"><strong data-start=3D"1760" data-e=
nd=3D"1787">Architecture logicielle</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1788" data-end=3D"1814">
<p data-start=3D"1790" data-end=3D"1814"><strong data-start=3D"1790" data-e=
nd=3D"1814">Design orient=C3=A9 objet</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1815" data-end=3D"1847">
<p data-start=3D"1817" data-end=3D"1847"><strong data-start=3D"1817" data-e=
nd=3D"1847">Documentation des syst=C3=A8mes</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1848" data-end=3D"1910">
<p data-start=3D"1850" data-end=3D"1910"><strong data-start=3D"1850" data-e=
nd=3D"1869">Tests logiciels</strong> (mod=C3=A9lisation de cas de test, sc=
=C3=A9narios)</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start=3D"1912" data-end=3D"1915">
<h2 data-start=3D"1917" data-end=3D"1955">=F0=9F=93=9A 2. Historique et =C3=
=89volution d=E2=80=99UML</h2>
<h3 data-start=3D"1957" data-end=3D"1969">Origines</h3>
<p data-start=3D"1970" data-end=3D"2129">Dans les ann=C3=A9es 1990, l=E2=80=
=99<strong data-start=3D"1994" data-end=3D"2018">industrie logicielle</stro=
ng> souffrait d=E2=80=99un manque de standardisation dans la mod=C3=A9lisat=
ion des logiciels. Plusieurs m=C3=A9thodes coexistaient :</p>
<ul data-start=3D"2131" data-end=3D"2284">
<li data-start=3D"2131" data-end=3D"2185">
<p data-start=3D"2133" data-end=3D"2185"><strong data-start=3D"2133" data-e=
nd=3D"2140">OMT</strong> (Object Modeling Technique =E2=80=93 James Rumbaug=
h)</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"2186" data-end=3D"2218">
<p data-start=3D"2188" data-end=3D"2218"><strong data-start=3D"2188" data-e=
nd=3D"2204">Booch method</strong> (Grady Booch)</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"2219" data-end=3D"2284">
<p data-start=3D"2221" data-end=3D"2284"><strong data-start=3D"2221" data-e=
nd=3D"2229">OOSE</strong> (Object-Oriented Software Engineering =E2=80=93 I=
var Jacobson)</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"2286" data-end=3D"2446">Chaque m=C3=A9thode avait ses prop=
res notations, concepts et outils. Cette diversit=C3=A9 cr=C3=A9ait <strong=
 data-start=3D"2373" data-end=3D"2428">de la confusion et des probl=C3=A8me=
s d=E2=80=99interop=C3=A9rabilit=C3=A9</strong> dans les =C3=A9quipes.</p>
<hr data-start=3D"2448" data-end=3D"2451">
<h3 data-start=3D"2453" data-end=3D"2473">Naissance de UML</h3>
<p data-start=3D"2474" data-end=3D"2694">En 1995, <strong data-start=3D"248=
3" data-end=3D"2514">Rumbaugh, Booch et Jacobson</strong> (les =E2=80=9Ctro=
is amigos=E2=80=9D) ont collabor=C3=A9 chez <strong data-start=3D"2555" dat=
a-end=3D"2576">Rational Software</strong> pour <strong data-start=3D"2582" =
data-end=3D"2609">unifier leurs approches</strong>. Leur but : cr=C3=A9er u=
n <strong data-start=3D"2631" data-end=3D"2649">langage unique</strong> pou=
r mod=C3=A9liser les syst=C3=A8mes orient=C3=A9s objets.</p>
<ul data-start=3D"2696" data-end=3D"2925">
<li data-start=3D"2696" data-end=3D"2853">
<p data-start=3D"2698" data-end=3D"2853">En 1997, <strong data-start=3D"270=
7" data-end=3D"2742">l=E2=80=99OMG (Object Management Group)</strong>, un c=
onsortium de standardisation de technologies logicielles, adopte <strong da=
ta-start=3D"2813" data-end=3D"2824">UML 1.0</strong> comme <strong data-sta=
rt=3D"2831" data-end=3D"2852">standard officiel</strong>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"2854" data-end=3D"2925">
<p data-start=3D"2856" data-end=3D"2925">Depuis, UML est <strong data-start=
=3D"2872" data-end=3D"2894">maintenu par l=E2=80=99OMG</strong> et a connu =
plusieurs versions.</p>
</li>
</ul>
