veaux : <strong data-start=3D"240" data-end=3D"277">d=C3=A9butant, interm=
=C3=A9diaire et expert</strong>.</p>
<hr data-start=3D"280" data-end=3D"283">
<h1 data-start=3D"285" data-end=3D"397">=F0=9F=A7=AD UML =E2=80=93 Diagramm=
e de Temps : Organisation de sc=C3=A9narios complexes, fragments d=E2=80=99=
interaction et contr=C3=B4le de flux</h1>
<hr data-start=3D"399" data-end=3D"402">
<h2 data-start=3D"404" data-end=3D"430">=F0=9F=93=8C Objectif du tutoriel</=
h2>
<p data-start=3D"432" data-end=3D"477">Le but de ce module est de vous appr=
endre =C3=A0 :</p>
<ul data-start=3D"479" data-end=3D"777">
<li data-start=3D"479" data-end=3D"551">
<p data-start=3D"481" data-end=3D"551">Organiser des <strong data-start=3D"=
495" data-end=3D"518">sc=C3=A9narios complexes</strong> dans des diagrammes=
 de temps UML</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"552" data-end=3D"672">
<p data-start=3D"554" data-end=3D"672">Utiliser les <strong data-start=3D"5=
67" data-end=3D"594">fragments d=E2=80=99interaction</strong> pour repr=C3=
=A9senter les comportements alternatifs, optionnels, ou concurrentiels</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"673" data-end=3D"777">
<p data-start=3D"675" data-end=3D"777">Appliquer des <strong data-start=3D"=
689" data-end=3D"732">m=C3=A9canismes de contr=C3=B4le de flux temporel</st=
rong> dans les syst=C3=A8mes temps r=C3=A9el ou multi-agents</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start=3D"779" data-end=3D"782">
<h2 data-start=3D"784" data-end=3D"829">=F0=9F=A7=B1 1. Rappels =E2=80=93 B=
ase du diagramme de temps</h2>
<p data-start=3D"831" data-end=3D"904">Avant de plonger dans la complexit=
=C3=A9, un rappel sur les <strong data-start=3D"886" data-end=3D"902">fonda=
mentaux</strong> :</p>
<ul data-start=3D"906" data-end=3D"1161">
<li data-start=3D"906" data-end=3D"934">
<p data-start=3D"908" data-end=3D"934">Axe horizontal =3D <strong data-star=
t=3D"925" data-end=3D"934">temps</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"935" data-end=3D"982">
<p data-start=3D"937" data-end=3D"982">Axe vertical =3D <strong data-start=
=3D"952" data-end=3D"982">objets, entit=C3=A9s ou signaux</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"983" data-end=3D"1074">
<p data-start=3D"985" data-end=3D"1074">Chaque <strong data-start=3D"992" d=
ata-end=3D"1004">lifeline</strong> montre l=E2=80=99=C3=A9volution de l=E2=
=80=99=C3=A9tat ou de la valeur d=E2=80=99un objet dans le temps</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1075" data-end=3D"1161">
<p data-start=3D"1077" data-end=3D"1161">Utilis=C3=A9 pour visualiser <stro=
ng data-start=3D"1101" data-end=3D"1155">les changements d=E2=80=99=C3=A9ta=
t, les =C3=A9v=C3=A9nements, les d=C3=A9lais</strong>, etc.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start=3D"1163" data-end=3D"1166">
<h2 data-start=3D"1168" data-end=3D"1218">=F0=9F=A7=A9 2. Pourquoi organise=
r un sc=C3=A9nario complexe&nbsp;?</h2>
<p data-start=3D"1220" data-end=3D"1281">Un diagramme de temps peut devenir=
 difficile =C3=A0 lire lorsque :</p>
<ul data-start=3D"1283" data-end=3D"1601">
<li data-start=3D"1283" data-end=3D"1369">
<p data-start=3D"1285" data-end=3D"1369">Plusieurs entit=C3=A9s sont impliq=
u=C3=A9es dans des <strong data-start=3D"1328" data-end=3D"1369">interactio=
ns parall=C3=A8les ou imbriqu=C3=A9es</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1370" data-end=3D"1436">
<p data-start=3D"1372" data-end=3D"1436">Il y a des <strong data-start=3D"1=
383" data-end=3D"1399">alternatives</strong> de comportements (choix, condi=
tions)</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1437" data-end=3D"1529">
<p data-start=3D"1439" data-end=3D"1529">Des sc=C3=A9narios doivent s=E2=80=
=99adapter =C3=A0 des <strong data-start=3D"1477" data-end=3D"1529">variati=
ons temporelles ou =C3=A9v=C3=A9nements asynchrones</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1530" data-end=3D"1601">
<p data-start=3D"1532" data-end=3D"1601">L=E2=80=99on veut <strong data-sta=
rt=3D"1542" data-end=3D"1601">g=C3=A9n=C3=A9rer plusieurs cas de test =C3=
=A0 partir d=E2=80=99un m=C3=AAme mod=C3=A8le</strong></p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"1603" data-end=3D"1737">=E2=9E=A1=EF=B8=8F D=E2=80=99o=C3=
=B9 la n=C3=A9cessit=C3=A9 de structurer le diagramme =C3=A0 l=E2=80=99aide=
 de <strong data-start=3D"1663" data-end=3D"1690">fragments d=E2=80=99inter=
action</strong>, h=C3=A9rit=C3=A9s en partie des diagrammes de s=C3=A9quenc=
e.</p>
<hr data-start=3D"1739" data-end=3D"1742">
<h2 data-start=3D"1744" data-end=3D"1789">=F0=9F=A7=AE 3. Les fragments d=
=E2=80=99interaction dans UML</h2>
<p data-start=3D"1791" data-end=3D"2120">Les <strong data-start=3D"1795" da=
ta-end=3D"1822">fragments d=E2=80=99interaction</strong> permettent de <str=
ong data-start=3D"1837" data-end=3D"1891">composer ou structurer des compor=
tements complexes</strong>. Bien qu=E2=80=99originellement utilis=C3=A9s da=
ns les <strong data-start=3D"1934" data-end=3D"1960">diagrammes de s=C3=A9q=
uence</strong>, certains peuvent aussi s=E2=80=99appliquer aux <strong data=
-start=3D"2001" data-end=3D"2024">diagrammes de temps</strong>, en particul=
ier pour <strong data-start=3D"2046" data-end=3D"2095">organiser visuelleme=
nt et logiquement le flux</strong> des =C3=A9tats et =C3=A9v=C3=A9nements.<=
/p>
